provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "mybucket" {
  bucket = "aws.sudheer.2026"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "bucketversioning" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.mybucket.id
  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Enabled"
  }
}

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "aws.sudheer.2026"
    key    = "new/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Think of S3 storage classes like storing your clothes:

1. S3 Standard (Daily Wear 👕)

You wear these clothes every day, so they stay in your cupboard.

Access: Very frequent
Cost: Highest
Example: Website images, application data

2. S3 Standard-IA (Occasional Wear 👔)

You wear these clothes once a month, but still want them nearby.

Access: Infrequent
Cost: Lower than Standard
Example: Backups, old project files

3. S3 One Zone-IA (Stored in One Room 🚪)

Same as above, but stored in only one room instead of multiple rooms.

Access: Infrequent
Cost: Cheaper
Example: Re-creatable data

4. S3 Intelligent-Tiering (Smart Wardrobe 🤖)

A smart cupboard automatically moves clothes based on how often you use them.

AWS automatically optimizes cost.
Example: Data with unknown access patterns.

5. Glacier Instant Retrieval (Locker 🔒)

Things you rarely need but want immediately when required.

Access: Milliseconds
Example: Medical records, archived documents

6. Glacier Flexible Retrieval (Storage Room 📦)

You can get the item back, but it takes some time.

Access: Minutes to Hours
Example: Old backups

7. Glacier Deep Archive (Bank Vault 🏦)

Very old items kept for years.

Cheapest storage
Retrieval: Hours
Example: Legal records, compliance data
Easy Interview Example

Suppose a company stores:

Current website images → S3 Standard
6-month-old backups → S3 Standard-IA
Unknown usage logs → S3 Intelligent-Tiering
5-year-old records → Glacier Deep Archive

This is the simplest real-world use of S3 storage classes.
